The Security Council,

Recalling all its previous resolutions and statements of its President concerning the situation in Somalia, in particular resolutions 2713 (2023), 2762 (2024), and 2776 (2025),

Reaffirming its full respect for the sovereignty, territorial integrity, political independence, and unity of the Federal Republic of Somalia,

Condemning in the strongest terms the continued attacks, atrocities, and destabilizing activities perpetrated by the terrorist group Al-Shabaab against civilians, Somali security forces, African Union personnel, and international partners, and recognizing the persistent threat that Al-Shabaab poses to peace, stability, and security in Somalia and the wider region,

Welcoming the continued commitment and progress made by the Federal Government of Somalia, in coordination with the Federal Member States, to combat terrorism, degrade Al-Shabaab, advance the security transition, and strengthen weapons and ammunition management,

Expressing grave concern at the continued illicit flow of weapons, military equipment, and ammunition into Somalia, particularly illicit maritime shipments originating from Yemen, and further expressing deep concern regarding documented links, cooperation, and illicit arms transfers between Al-Shabaab and the Houthis, which threaten regional peace, security, and maritime navigation,

Noting with concern incidents of maritime insecurity, armed robbery, and attacks on commercial shipping off the coast of Somalia, and emphasizing the necessity of safeguarding international navigation and commerce in regional waters,

Recognizing that targeted sanctions remain an essential tool in disrupting Al-Shabaab’s access to illicit weapons, components for improvised explosive devices (IEDs), and financing, while reaffirming that sanctions measures are not intended to impede the legitimate development of Somalia’s national security institutions or to create adverse humanitarian consequences,

Recalling resolution 2664 (2022) and emphasizing the continued applicability of the humanitarian carve-out to the measures reaffirmed herein,

Determining that the situation in Somalia, driven by the activities of Al-Shabaab, continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to renew until 15 December 2026 the measures on Al-Shabaab, including the targeted arms embargo, travel ban, and asset freeze, first imposed in resolution 1844 (2008) and modified in subsequent relevant resolutions, including resolution 2713 (2023) and resolution 2776 (2025);

2. Reaffirms that the arms embargo imposed pursuant to paragraph 1 of this resolution shall apply exclusively to Al-Shabaab and to non-state actors operating in Somalia without the authorization or approval of the Federal Government of Somalia, and does not apply to weapons, ammunition, or military equipment destined for the sole use of the Federal Government of Somalia or its security forces;

3. Decides to extend until 15 December 2026 the authorizations granted to Member States, acting nationally or through regional naval partnerships, to inspect vessels in Somali territorial waters and on the high seas off the coast of Somalia, extending to and including the Arabian Sea and the Gulf of Aden, which they have reasonable grounds to believe are carrying illicit arms, ammunition, and military equipment destined for or originating from Al-Shabaab, components for improvised explosive devices (IEDs), or charcoal in violation of relevant Security Council resolutions, and calls upon all inspecting states to act in full accordance with international law, including the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS), and with the notification requirements set out in resolution 2713 (2023);

4. Reaffirms the ban on the import and export of Somali charcoal and the restrictions on the supply, sale, or transfer of precursor chemicals and components used for improvised explosive devices, as set out in resolution 2713 (2023) and updated in resolution 2776 (2025), and calls upon all Member States to enhance customs vigilance, supply chain transparency, and export controls to disrupt Al-Shabaab's access to such dual-use materials;

5. Urges Member States, particularly regional states and flag states, to strengthen cooperation, maritime information-sharing, and operational coordination to disrupt illicit maritime smuggling routes between Yemen and Somalia, and directs the Sanctions Committee established pursuant to resolution 2713 (2023) and the Sanctions Committee established pursuant to resolution 2140 (2014) to enhance coordination, exchange information, and monitor trends concerning cooperation and weapons transfers between the Houthis and Al-Shabaab;

6. Decides to extend the mandate of the Panel of Experts on Somalia, as described in paragraph 31 of resolution 2713 (2023) and reaffirmed in resolution 2776 (2025), until 15 January 2027, and expresses its intention to review the mandate and take appropriate action regarding any further extension no later than 15 December 2026;

7. Requests the Panel of Experts to provide to the Security Council, through the Committee, a midterm update no later than 15 June 2026, and a final report no later than 15 October 2026;

8. Urges the Federal Government of Somalia, Member States, the United Nations Assistance Mission in Somalia (and its successor transitional configurations), the African Union Transition Mission in Somalia (and its successor mission, the African Union Support and Stabilization Mission in Somalia), and other relevant international and regional entities to cooperate fully with the Panel of Experts and ensure the safety and unhindered access of its members;

9. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

